__registerContent("newspaper/polity/2026-08-28/obc-creamy-layer", {"notes":{"news-2026-08-28-obc-creamy-layer":{"newsType":true,"date":"28 August 2026","paper":"The Hindu","subject":"Polity / GS-2 (Reservation, Social Justice, OBCs)","summary":"The Parliamentary Standing Committee on the Welfare of OBCs (chaired by BJP MP Ganesh Singh) has flagged that the operationalisation of OBC 'creamy layer' exclusion is stuck between two ministries — the Ministry of Personnel (DoPT) and the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment. Two components are unresolved: (1) the income/wealth test and (2) the 'equivalence of posts'. The panel found the DoPT was practising 'hostile discrimination' by applying the income test differently to OBC candidates whose parents are in government service versus those whose parents are in jobs where post-equivalence has not been established. The committee has urged that salaries be excluded from the income test (consistent with the Supreme Court's reading in Indra Sawhney that salary and agricultural income are not counted), and that the equivalence-of-posts framework be finalised. A 2004 DoPT letter was reportedly being interpreted incorrectly, and the matter has been pending since a 2015 report.","keyPoints":[{"heading":"The Two Unresolved Components","points":["Income/wealth test: the annual income ceiling used to identify the 'creamy layer' among OBCs","Equivalence of posts: rules to treat parents in the private/organised sector on par with government posts for creamy-layer determination","Both are stuck between the Ministry of Personnel (DoPT) and the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment"]},{"heading":"The Panel's Findings","points":["The DoPT was 'practising hostile discrimination' by applying the income test differently to OBC candidates with parents in government service vs those in jobs where post-equivalence is not established","The committee directed the government to EXCLUDE salaries from the income test","It noted a 2004 DoPT letter (Office Memorandum) was being interpreted incorrectly","The equivalence framework had 'not been established' and remained pending"]},{"heading":"The Creamy Layer Principle","points":["The Supreme Court's reading: salary and agricultural income are NOT to be counted while computing income for creamy-layer status","Only 'other income' (e.g. from property, business) is considered against the income ceiling","The committee found implementing the court's reading 'extremely difficult' where equivalence had not been established"]},{"heading":"Institutional Angle","points":["The Cabinet Minister in charge of the Personnel Ministry (DoPT) is the Prime Minister","The Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment is the nodal ministry for OBC welfare","The Parliamentary Standing Committee on Welfare of OBCs is chaired by Ganesh Singh (BJP MP)","Both ministries have been asked to respond to the committee"]}],"importantTerms":[{"term":"Creamy Layer","explanation":"The relatively advanced/wealthy sections among OBCs who are excluded from reservation benefits. The concept was laid down in Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992). The current income ceiling is ₹8 lakh per annum, and salary and agricultural income are excluded from the computation."},{"term":"Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992)","explanation":"The 'Mandal Commission case' in which the Supreme Court upheld 27% reservation for OBCs, capped total reservation at 50%, and mandated exclusion of the creamy layer — while ruling that salary and agricultural income are not to be counted for the income test."},{"term":"DoPT (Department of Personnel and Training)","explanation":"Under the Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions, the DoPT frames rules on recruitment and reservation in central services. Its 2004 Office Memorandum on the income test is at the centre of this dispute."},{"term":"Equivalence of Posts","explanation":"A framework to determine whether a parent's post in the private/organised sector is 'equivalent' to a government post, for deciding creamy-layer status — needed to treat candidates uniformly. Its non-finalisation is the core administrative gap flagged by the panel."}],"mainsAnswerNotes":["The dispute illustrates how administrative inertia and inter-ministerial coordination failures can dilute a constitutionally mandated safeguard (creamy-layer exclusion), producing unequal treatment among similarly placed OBC candidates.","Applying the income test differently based on the sector of a parent's employment risks violating Article 14 (equality) and Article 16 (equality of opportunity in public employment).","The Indra Sawhney principle — excluding salary and agricultural income — is designed to identify genuine social/economic advancement, not merely high earnings; inconsistent application defeats this purpose.","Parliamentary committees play a vital oversight role: here the OBC Welfare Committee has surfaced an implementation gap and pushed the executive toward resolution.","Way forward: finalise the equivalence-of-posts framework, issue a corrected, uniform income-test instruction, and periodically revise the income ceiling to preserve the intent of reservation."],"prelims":["Creamy layer concept: laid down in Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992), the Mandal case","Indra Sawhney also capped total reservation at 50% and upheld 27% OBC reservation","Creamy-layer income ceiling: ₹8 lakh per annum (revised periodically)","Salary and agricultural income are EXCLUDED from the creamy-layer income computation","DoPT is under the Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions (Minister-in-charge: the Prime Minister)","Nodal ministry for OBC welfare: Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment","National Commission for Backward Classes got constitutional status via the 102nd Amendment (Articles 338B, 342A)"]}},"quizzes":{"news-2026-08-28-obc-creamy-layer":{"title":"OBC Creamy Layer Income Test","questions":[{"q":"The concept of the 'creamy layer' among OBCs was laid down by the Supreme Court in which case?","options":["Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973)","Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992)","M. Nagaraj v. Union of India (2006)","Champakam Dorairajan v. State of Madras (1951)"],"correct":1,"explain":"The creamy-layer concept was laid down in Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992), the Mandal Commission case, which also upheld 27% OBC reservation and the 50% ceiling."},{"q":"According to the Supreme Court's reading, which of the following is EXCLUDED while computing income for creamy-layer status?","options":["Income from property","Salary and agricultural income","Business income","Rental income"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Court held that salary and agricultural income are not to be counted; only other income (property, business, etc.) is considered against the ceiling."},{"q":"The two components of OBC creamy-layer exclusion that are 'stuck between ministries' are:","options":["Caste census and headcount","The income/wealth test and the equivalence of posts","Reservation percentage and roster system","Domicile and language criteria"],"correct":1,"explain":"The article identifies the income/wealth test and the equivalence of posts as the two unresolved components."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. The Parliamentary Standing Committee on Welfare of OBCs flagged the issue.","II. The committee found the DoPT was practising 'hostile discrimination' in applying the income test.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — the OBC Welfare Committee (chaired by Ganesh Singh) flagged the issue and used the phrase 'hostile discrimination' about the DoPT's differential application."},{"q":"The two ministries between which the issue is stuck are the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment and the:","options":["Ministry of Home Affairs","Ministry of Personnel (DoPT)","Ministry of Law and Justice","Ministry of Education"],"correct":1,"explain":"The issue is stuck between the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment and the Ministry of Personnel (which houses the DoPT)."},{"q":"What is the current annual income ceiling for determining the OBC creamy layer?","options":["₹2.5 lakh","₹6 lakh","₹8 lakh","₹10 lakh"],"correct":2,"explain":"The creamy-layer income ceiling is currently ₹8 lakh per annum (revised periodically), excluding salary and agricultural income."},{"q":"The 'hostile discrimination' identified by the panel arose because the income test was applied differently based on:","options":["The religion of the candidate","Whether the candidate's parents were in government service or in jobs where post-equivalence was not established","The state of domicile of the candidate","The candidate's own income"],"correct":1,"explain":"The DoPT applied the income test differently to OBC candidates with parents in government service versus those whose parents' posts had no established equivalence — producing unequal treatment."},{"q":"Assertion (A): Applying the income test differently to similarly placed OBC candidates can be constitutionally problematic.\nReason (R): Articles 14 and 16 guarantee equality and equality of opportunity in public employment.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R explains A — unequal application of the income test to similarly placed candidates offends the equality guarantees of Articles 14 and 16."},{"q":"The DoPT document reportedly being interpreted incorrectly dates from which year?","options":["1993","2004","2015","2025"],"correct":1,"explain":"The article states a 2004 DoPT letter (Office Memorandum) on the income test was being interpreted incorrectly."},{"q":"The Indra Sawhney judgment capped total reservation at what percentage?","options":["27%","33%","50%","60%"],"correct":2,"explain":"Indra Sawhney (1992) capped total reservation at 50%, while upholding 27% reservation for OBCs."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about the creamy-layer income test:","I. Only income other than salary and agricultural income is counted.","II. The committee found it 'extremely difficult' to implement the court's reading where post-equivalence was not established.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — only 'other income' is counted, and the committee flagged the difficulty of applying this where equivalence of posts had not been established."},{"q":"The Parliamentary Standing Committee on the Welfare of OBCs is chaired by:","options":["Virendra Kumar","Ganesh Singh","Narendra Modi","S.Y. Quraishi"],"correct":1,"explain":"The committee is chaired by BJP MP Ganesh Singh."},{"q":"Which Ministry is the nodal ministry for the welfare of OBCs?","options":["Ministry of Personnel","Ministry of Home Affairs","Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment","Ministry of Tribal Affairs"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment is the nodal ministry for OBC welfare (Tribal Affairs handles STs)."},{"q":"The National Commission for Backward Classes (NCBC) was granted constitutional status by which amendment?","options":["102nd Amendment","103rd Amendment","105th Amendment","106th Amendment"],"correct":0,"explain":"The 102nd Constitutional Amendment (2018) inserted Articles 338B and 342A, granting the NCBC constitutional status."},{"q":"The 'equivalence of posts' framework is needed to:","options":["Decide the reservation percentage for OBCs","Determine whether a parent's non-government post is equivalent to a government post for creamy-layer status","Fix the salaries of government employees","Conduct the caste census"],"correct":1,"explain":"Equivalence of posts determines whether a parent's private/organised-sector post is on par with a government post, so that creamy-layer status is decided uniformly."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. The Minister-in-charge of the Personnel Ministry is the Prime Minister.","II. The creamy-layer principle was introduced to exclude the advanced sections among OBCs from reservation.","III. Salary is fully counted while computing creamy-layer income.","How many of the statements are correct?"],"options":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"correct":1,"explain":"Statements I and II are correct. III is wrong — salary (and agricultural income) is EXCLUDED from the creamy-layer income computation."},{"q":"The 27% reservation for OBCs upheld in Indra Sawhney was based on the recommendations of which commission?","options":["Kaka Kalelkar Commission","Mandal Commission","Sachar Committee","Rohini Commission"],"correct":1,"explain":"The 27% OBC reservation stems from the Second Backward Classes Commission — the Mandal Commission — upheld in Indra Sawhney (1992)."},{"q":"The core reason the creamy-layer operationalisation remains unresolved, per the article, is:","options":["A pending Supreme Court verdict","Inter-ministerial coordination failure and non-finalisation of the equivalence framework","Lack of parliamentary interest","Absence of any income data"],"correct":1,"explain":"The issue is stuck due to coordination failure between the DoPT and the Social Justice Ministry and the non-finalisation of the equivalence-of-posts framework."},{"q":"Which body identifies and recommends inclusion/exclusion of communities in the OBC central list?","options":["The Election Commission","The National Commission for Backward Classes (NCBC)","The Finance Commission","The Law Commission"],"correct":1,"explain":"The National Commission for Backward Classes (a constitutional body since the 102nd Amendment) examines and recommends on the central OBC list."},{"q":"The Rohini Commission, associated with OBC reservation, was constituted to examine:","options":["The creamy-layer income ceiling","Sub-categorisation of OBCs for more equitable distribution of reservation benefits","Reservation in promotions","The 50% ceiling"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Rohini Commission was constituted to examine the sub-categorisation of OBCs so that reservation benefits are distributed more equitably among backward communities."}]}}});
